sit-proxy: keep what the CP says before a client is listening

The CP talks as soon as the driver releases it, and it does not repeat itself.
Its carrier-configuration handshake is the case that matters: it pushes
CARRIER_CONFIG_SIM_INFO once, naming the SIM it wants a configuration for, and
waits to be pointed at one with CARRIER_CONFIG_MANIFEST. Miss that indication
and the CP applies no carrier policy at all -- it then keeps no URSP rules, and
from that point refuses every PDN but the one it attached with, answering
MATCH_ALL_RULE_NOT_ALLOWED (2253). The visible result is a modem that
registers, carries data, and cannot place a VoLTE call, which is what made this
look like an IMS problem for a month.

This proxy was dropping those frames. It binds its socket and opens the device
before any client exists, so for the first seconds of a boot there is a
listener on the device with nobody behind it, and the indication arm had no
case for that: with no peer to broadcast to, the frame fell off the end. On
komodo the window is five seconds -- sit-proxy opens the ports at boot and
ModemManager connects five seconds later -- and CARRIER_CONFIG_SIM_INFO has
never once been seen by ModemManager as a result.

So keep them and hand them to the first client that takes indications, in frame
order. Bounded, because a boot where no client ever arrives must not grow
without end, and naturally discarded when the device is reopened, since
indications from a CP that has restarted describe a state that no longer
exists.

